From 9f40b2a9425babd3e70a71fd86d3fed70ecbcd6d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Aidan <eltorago@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 13 Sep 2026 03:14:09 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/3] Round-trip the corpus against the source documents
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

tools/verify_against_source.py looks for every stored control in the document it
came from, and reports order-preserving word coverage rather than a contiguous
match. Contiguity is the wrong test: a maturity marker, a footnote digit or a
running header sits between two words of a control on the page, and text that is
entirely present fails a substring search. Calibrated on C2M2, whose extractor was
already checked against the document's own count of 356, until it read 356 of 356.

What it found, and what is fixed here:

- 22 ZTMM stage texts read "enterprise- wide", "password- less", "case-by- case".
  A PDF has no line-break character, so joining a wrapped line with a space breaks
  the word. tools/textjoin.py mends it, keeping the hyphen and dropping the space,
  because every break of this kind in these documents falls on a hyphen the
  publisher writes. A suspended hyphen — NIST's "security- and privacy-related" —
  is left alone, and there is a case for that so the rule cannot lose the
  distinction.
- 4 ZTMM stage texts carried a footnote number mid-sentence: "policies 26 for the
  lifecycle", "internal and 28 external traffic". Superscript digits, dropped by
  size the way the 800-57 welded footnotes were.
- 10 more broken words across six extractors: oag-wa, pspf, c2m2, asd-ad, 800-88
  and the NIST PDF route.

Three cases in tests/test_extracts.py and two matrix entries. The suite reads all
5,321 controls for the broken-word rule, so it cannot pass on an empty set.

Round-trip after the fix: 4,584 texts, 3,713 verbatim, 399 joined, 34 near, 379
partial, 59 absent. ZTMM is declared unreadable by this method rather than counted,
because pdfplumber cannot read its tables in order and all forty came back absent
when they are all present.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
